;B[ar];W[bs];B[dm];W[fn];B[ki]C[leachy [4k\]\: how can one semi-reliably calculate the value of moves in oyose here...is there a known technique or is it just judgement using experience
leachy [4k\]\: ?
]
;W[kh];B[qk];W[ri];B[pf];W[of];B[jm];W[in];B[hm];W[rg];B[rf]C[pojki [4d\]\: the most common way is to count the points when you play there first and when your opponennt plays there first, then the difference is the value
]
;W[im];B[il];W[mo];B[mp];W[ee];B[ef];W[dd];B[ce];W[kl];B[jl];W[ml];B[lm];W[ll];B[fe]
;W[ed];B[mj]C[leachy [4k\]\: but then there is the follow-ups and possible ignoring to tenuki etc. does it get any more sophisticated than that?
]
;W[nj];B[go];W[gp];B[pg];W[ph];B[pd];W[oe];B[pk]C[pojki [4d\]\: it is usualy easy to count with the best follow up for both sides. this method works quite good for evaluating gote moves. when evaluating sente (like when you play some move your opponent would have in sente), there is always a problem with the rest of the board as you must estimate how much sente is worth
]
;W[ih];B[hh]C[leachy [4k\]\: thanks very much for your answer there. play at this stage always is a bit of a mystery to me and yet it seems to me that some complex mathematical algorithm should give a very good idea
]
